---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/voltage-drop';
import { GAUGES, gaugeLabel, RESISTANCE, voltageDrop, type Gauge } from '../lib/electrical';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const maxFt = (g: Gauge, amps: number, volts: number) => {
  const vdPerFt = voltageDrop('cu', g, amps, 1, '1')!;
  return (volts * 0.03) / vdPerFt;
};
const rows = (['14', '12', '10', '8', '6', '4'] as Gauge[]).map((g) => ({ g, a15: maxFt(g, 15, 120), a20: maxFt(g, 20, 120), a30: maxFt(g, 30, 240) }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="voltage-drop-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Voltage Drop Calculator: Copper & Aluminum, 1- and 3-Phase"
  description="Free voltage drop calculator. Enter wire size, amps, one-way distance, and voltage to get the voltage drop in volts and percent, using NEC Chapter 9 resistance values."
  h1="Voltage Drop Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Check how much voltage a circuit loses over its length, in volts and percent, for copper or aluminum wire on single- or
    three-phase circuits.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="formula">Voltage drop formula</h2>
  <p class="formula">single phase: VD = 2 × L × I × R ÷ 1,000</p>
  <p class="formula">three phase: VD = 1.732 × L × I × R ÷ 1,000</p>
  <p>
    L is the one-way length in feet, I the current in amps, and R the conductor resistance in ohms per 1,000 feet. The
    single-phase formula uses 2 because current travels out and back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="example">Example: 12 AWG copper, 20 A, 100 ft, 120 V</h2>
  <p>
    12 AWG copper is 1.93 Ω per 1,000 ft. 2 × 100 × 20 × 1.93 ÷ 1,000 = 7.72 V, which is <strong>6.4% of 120 V</strong>,
    more than double the recommended 3%. Stepping up to 8 AWG (0.764 Ω) brings it to 3.06 V, or 2.5%.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">How much voltage drop is acceptable?</h2>
  <p>
    The NEC recommends, in informational notes, no more than 3% on a branch circuit and 5% total for feeder plus branch
    circuit. These aren't enforceable code limits in most places, but equipment runs better within them. Motors run hot
    and electronics can reset when voltage sags further.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Maximum run for 3% drop, copper</h2>
  <p>One-way distance, single phase.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Wire</th><th class="num">15 A at 120 V</th><th class="num">20 A at 120 V</th><th class="num">30 A at 240 V</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr><td>{gaugeLabel(r.g)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.a15, 0)} ft</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.a20, 0)} ft</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.a30, 0)} ft</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="resistance">Conductor resistance</h2>
  <p>NEC Chapter 9, Table 8, DC resistance at 75°C, ohms per 1,000 feet.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Size</th><th class="num">Copper</th><th class="num">Aluminum</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{GAUGES.map((g) => <tr><td>{gaugeLabel(g)}</td><td class="num">{RESISTANCE.cu[g]}</td><td class="num">{RESISTANCE.al[g] ?? '—'}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="dc">Voltage drop on DC circuits</h2>
  <p>
    DC uses the single-phase formula, because current flows out on one wire and back on the other. Low-voltage systems
    feel it most: a 0.77 V drop is under 1% at 120 V but 6.4% at 12 V. Choose <em>DC</em> in the calculator for solar,
    RV, and marine circuits.
  </p>

  <p>
    Running several circuits in one pipe? <a href="/conduit-fill-calculator/">Check the conduit fill</a> before you pull wire.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Is the distance one way or round trip?</h3>
  <p>One way, from the panel to the load. The formula doubles it for single-phase circuits.</p>
  <h3>How do I reduce voltage drop?</h3>
  <p>
    Use a larger wire, shorten the run, or run the load at a higher voltage: a 240 V circuit carries half the current of a
    120 V circuit for the same power, and its drop is a smaller percentage.
  </p>
  <p>
    To pick a wire that meets both ampacity and voltage drop at once, use the
    <a href="/wire-size-calculator/">wire size calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Resistance values from NEC Chapter 9 Table 8 (uncoated copper and aluminum, 75°C).</li>
    <li>DC resistance is used; for AC circuits over about 1/0 AWG, reactance adds slightly to the drop.</li>
    <li>Results are for planning; confirm designs with your local code and a licensed electrician.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
